17592186045417] [17592186149313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10460ef1-9c6c-5096-be9c-020abc2e4cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149314 #edb/ref 1080 "{(1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (0, 1), (2, 1)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149314 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149314 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "596a57c7-66aa-5fed-8f11-f1534ccf2c87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151817 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151817 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\nII. $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841e5dcb-c092-5159-bfc4-d6580999c742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151817 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{2n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{2(1),2(2),2(3),…\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…\\} \\\\  & =\\{\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151818 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151818 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{ v, w \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea87c6f2-d0ef-5f8d-8f87-b9219272cedd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151818 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}\\}⊔\\{v,w\\} & =\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}\\}\\cup \\{v,w\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151819 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151819 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{ 7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{ 2,4,8 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65852684-d9ce-5830-b95f-9abae3dadd4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151819 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(7,1),(8,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{7,8\\}⊔\\{2,4,8\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(7,1),(8,1)\\}\\cup \\{(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(7,1),(8,1),(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152101 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152101 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152101 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152101 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [