ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1061 157023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0fe9f92-abdf-5afd-94bd-7e0416495b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1061 157024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7342dbf9-8375-507b-9fcc-03ddc4dd27a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1061 157027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "233824ca-a0f9-5e23-af03-ba0c108fc90c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1061 157028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d572a9fc-16a3-5783-b7e1-a3d5a6eab501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1064 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty:\n\n$$\nC\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1065 8004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92860310-1be6-50cf-8447-2b24a95db5e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1064 "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union. \n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since \n\n$$\n\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,\n$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{0,2,4\\}⊔\\{1,3\\} & =\\{0,2,4\\}\\cup \\{1,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1065 8005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d264ae6-080d-5ca0-be06-b6d41a5f4aa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1067 "The Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, consider the following overlapping sets: \n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.\n$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. \n\n$$\nA_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTo construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{◻,△\\}⊔\\{♡,◻\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\}\\cup \\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1065 8137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e3b30b0-95ac-5b38-a7e6-169cc3409776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1067 "The Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374255 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5208a32e-7d59-5ee5-9042-e86e28dacbd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374256 #edb/ref 1080 "∅" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f0b39b3-d983-513a-9d6d-a04ceae30f65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374257 #edb/ref 1080 "{a,b,c,1,2,3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3a81384-2101-5389-a6f3-63eabe2338c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374258 #edb/ref 1080 "{(a,1),(b,1),(c,1),(a,2),(b,2),(c,2)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374258 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374258 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7ebfdce-f2cc-5e28-a586-3c7c26f6925b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374259 #edb/ref 1080 "{a,b,c}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374259 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374259 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48377b1f-e9ff-5cbe-9cf4-0641c34d21fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374260 #edb/ref 1080 "{a,b,c,1,2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374260 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374260 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c33fa6a-fed2-553f-aa5f-447bec97d177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472086 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472087 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472088 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nI. $\\{3,4,5\\}$ and $\\{6,7,8\\}$\n\nII. $\\{-4,-3,-2\\}$ and $\\{2,3,4\\}$\n\nIII. $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0311da7-2e05-528f-8234-394bbb366e6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{3,4,5\\}\\cap \\{6,7,8\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{-4,-3,-2\\}\\cap \\{2,3,4\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]=\\{0\\}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454528 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454529 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454530 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={2n∣n\\in N},\\,B={{2}^{n}∣n\\in N}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B={2,4,8,16,…}$\n- **II.** $A\\cap B=∅$\n- **III.** $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e6d5c0d-f5b8-5d41-acfe-dd83480488ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374282 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,16,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,8,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & ={2,4,6,8,…}\\text{ }\\cap {2,4,8,16,…} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,…} \\\\  & \\ne ∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the intersection is not empty, the two sets are not disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1071 "${3,5,7}⊔{0,6,12}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eefa7b71-9f96-5562-bf08-51d2f36fc7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374303 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n{3,5,7}\\cap {0,6,12}=∅.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3,5,7}⊔{0,6,12} & ={3,5,7}\\cup {0,6,12} \\\\  & ={0,3,5,6,7,12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374304 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374305 #edb